\chapter{I/O Subsystem and System Call Architecture}

\section{Completion-Based Asynchronous System Calls}

Rather than relying on synchronous trap-based system calls, Telix implements a non-blocking completion interface inspired by K42 \footcite{krie06} and Scheduler Activation upcalls \footcite{ande91}. System invocations are submitted to shared ring buffers, allowing user and kernel space to communicate asynchronously without forced privilege-level transitions.

\section{Zero-Copy Cross-Domain Buffering}

High-throughput networking and disk I/O use lock-free, zero-copy cross-domain Fast Buffers (Fbufs) \footcite{drus93}. Memory pages are pooled and transferred across user-kernel boundaries by swapping buffer ownership rather than copying byte payloads.
